00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:48.150
right now.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.350
Hopefully you won't
hear that too much.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.480
But thank you so
much for joining us.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:56.680
As the co-host today, Dara is a
data science manager at Stripe.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.110
And I'd love to have you
introduce yourself, maybe

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.300
share a little bit
about your role,

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.240
and also something you
like to do outside of work.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.110
Sure.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.060
So thank you for the intro.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.199
My name's Dara.

00:02:08.199 --> 00:02:10.640
As Rachael said, I'm a data
science manager at Stripe

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:11.140
currently.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.900
I've been at Stripe for
about five years now.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.480
My current role
is leading a team

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.890
around finance and
operations data science.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.550
So we are very into forecasting.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:24.040
We do a lot of work on
hierarchical forecasting,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:28.030
financial forecasting at Stripe,
spend a lot of effort there.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.930
We think a lot about metrics
and overall company metrics

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.360
and analytics and how we
can understand our business

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:35.620
performance.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:38.270
And then we work in the
operations space as well.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:42.460
So we are working on some
various machine learning

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.430
projects there, as well
as capacity planning

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:47.020
and forecasting
on that side too.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:52.070
So happy to talk about any
of that work in more detail.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:52.570
Let's see.

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:55.030
One thing I like to
do outside of work--

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.340
I used to be kind of
an endurance sports

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.650
person, long-distance
triathlons and running.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.240
But I now have a
15-month-old daughter,

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.790
who keeps me pretty busy
and spend most of my time

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:07.500
just parenting.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.070
Well, thank you so
much for joining us.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.590
As we kind of wait for questions
to come in from everybody

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.450
in the audience
here, I'm curious

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.750
if you could kind of give us
a little insight into some

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.580
of the problems
that your team is

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.440
trying to help solve or
things that you're working on

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.563
so you can put us in
your frame of reference.

00:03:25.563 --> 00:03:26.250
Cool.

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.140
Yeah, let me talk about
a couple of things.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.910
So at Stripe, we're
interested in forecasting,

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:35.037
as many businesses are, in
understanding how much payment

00:03:35.037 --> 00:03:36.620
volume are we going
to have next year,

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:40.310
how many active users will we
have, what will our uptime be.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:42.740
There's a number of
forecasting problems

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:46.430
that come into play that
are a business advantage.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.800
If we are good at them,
we can set the budget.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:49.970
We can set our targets.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:53.010
We can direct our
efforts appropriately.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.400
And so my team
spends a lot of time

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.780
thinking about
how we can produce

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:59.150
the best forecast for Stripe.

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:01.490
As a side note, I like
forecasting as a data science

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:03.680
area because it's not
one that you always

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.450
learn about in a
machine learning course

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:09.860
or spend a lot of time
thinking about in your more

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:10.880
formal education.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.260
I didn't, at least.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:15.710
But now, on the
business side, it's

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:19.533
an extremely practical business
problem to be able to solve.

00:04:19.533 --> 00:04:21.950
But at the same time, it's
very interesting statistically,

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:23.325
and there's a lot
of work that we

00:04:23.325 --> 00:04:26.510
do to experiment
and find the best

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:28.970
methods for doing forecasting.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:32.660
So we do what's called
hierarchical forecasting.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.600
So this means that we want
to have a forecast for Stripe

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:36.100
overall.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.308
We want to know what's our
total payment volume going

00:04:38.308 --> 00:04:39.660
to be for the whole company.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:40.850
But we also want
to know, what is it

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:41.990
going to be for each region?

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:43.610
What is it going to be
for each business type?

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:44.840
What's it going to
be for each cohort?

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:46.910
There's all these
dimensions that flow down.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.160
So you can kind of think of
this as a hierarchy, where

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:53.390
all of the dimensions then need
to add up to the total, right?

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:55.160
We have to have a
coherent hierarchy

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.090
is how we talk about it.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:00.590
And so we use this method
where we can actually

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:02.990
choose any forecasting
method we want

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:04.880
for each node of the hierarchy.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.370
We use a lot of classical
forecasting methods,

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.840
like ARIMA and
exponential smoothing.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.150
We also experiment with
other types of methods.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.330
And we use those
to make estimates.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.850
And then we apply sort of
a meta model on top of it,

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:21.110
hierarchical reconciliation, to
basically tweak each forecast

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:23.390
as little as possible
so that they all still

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:25.580
add up to the total forecast.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.030
And that, then, we apply
some additional adjustments

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:29.867
on top of that.

00:05:29.867 --> 00:05:31.700
And that becomes the
budget for the company.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:35.480
Thank you.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.780
I see Mark had a follow-up
question on that.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:40.640
Mark, do you want to
jump in and ask that?

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:41.140
Yeah.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:41.640
Sure.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:42.640
Let me turn my video on.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:48.070
Just curious about your--

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:50.050
and you kind of went
into the detail there.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:54.640
But curious about if you're able
to expand on the target setting

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:58.090
methodology and if
you dip into any,

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:01.630
I guess, nonfinancial
targets and, I

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:03.790
guess, different level setting.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:06.400
I actually literally
have it open

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.440
right now working on R,
next fiscal year's targets

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:14.320
for the hospital
system I work for.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.990
So, yeah, just really curious
to get your perspective

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:20.560
on some of the
nonfinancial metrics

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:22.900
that you guys keep track
of and if and how you

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:25.345
do [INAUDIBLE] for those.

00:06:25.345 --> 00:06:25.870
Mm-hmm.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:26.380
Yeah, we do.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:27.797
We're actually
building a platform

00:06:27.797 --> 00:06:31.570
to do general hierarchical
forecasting for any metric

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:32.140
at Stripe.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:33.550
So we've expanded this.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.080
We do some sales
planning right now,

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.200
which is a little bit financial.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.300
We do it for our active users.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:44.200
And we're looking at
things like uptime,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:49.940
as well, like how much should
we expect Stripe to be live?

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:54.320
And, yeah, so we generally
find that the approach is-- oh,

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.690
and we also do a lot in ops
too, so capacity planning

00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:59.300
for our support volume
support tickets.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.280
We found that the
methods are pretty

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.470
similar across all of these
different types of problems.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:07.850
There's a lot of nuance
in terms of making sure

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:10.853
that you capture the data
correctly and actually solve

00:07:10.853 --> 00:07:12.020
the business problem, right?

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:14.390
You can just sort of throw
general forecasting methods

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:15.800
at something.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:18.800
But we have found, in general,
that the hierarchical format

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:22.010
works really well in a business
setting, in that you often

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:23.750
care about some more
detailed dimensions,

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:25.917
and then you also care about
adding up to the whole.

00:07:25.917 --> 00:07:26.700
Mm-hmm.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:27.200
Awesome.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.400
Thank you.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.690
Crystal, I see you asked
a question in the chat too.

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:32.648
Do you want to jump in?

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:39.820
And maybe I missed--

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:41.210
I can ask that one too.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.210
And if you want to add
it in context, feel free.

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:48.100
But the question was, any
exploration of ChatGPT

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:51.460
to identify any possible
or meaningful use?

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:52.720
It's funny you ask.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:54.640
Yes, we are playing with it.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.010
We actually were playing
with it before it was cool,

00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:59.740
I have to say, so back
when it was just GPT3,

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:02.620
before some of the
hype this fall.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:06.700
We are experimenting with
it in our support flow,

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:10.210
so to be able to help
agents answer questions

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:13.670
more accurately and much
more quickly for our users.

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:17.050
So we are still early days,
but we're pretty enthusiastic.

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:20.200
We've done a lot of NLP work
generally on the op side.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.380
So we classify our
issues using BERT,

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:26.200
which is sort of a previous
generation natural language

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:27.790
processing model.

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:32.260
And that allows us to know
what kind of questions

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:34.840
are getting asked and what
topics our support issues are

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:36.700
about, which we
then use to direct

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:39.880
a lot of internal efforts,
like where should we be making

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:42.216
updates or bug
fixes in our product

00:08:42.216 --> 00:08:44.049
based on what the support
tickets are about.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:47.210
Thank you.

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:48.793
I want to make sure
I get over to some

00:08:48.793 --> 00:08:50.150
of the anonymous questions too.

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:52.790
And just remind people,
if you want to use Slido,

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:55.530
you can use that
link in the chat.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:58.700
But somebody had asked
earlier, I would love to learn,

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:00.590
Dara, how you have
found the transition

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:06.110
from computational immunology
to finance and operations?

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:07.310
Yeah.

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:12.500
I'll try to not blather on
for too long about this.

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:14.930
A lot of the data scientists
have interesting or unusual

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:16.370
career paths.

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:18.650
I'm no exception here.

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:22.700
So I did my PhD in
computational immunology.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:26.090
It actually started out as
a PhD in pure immunology.

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:29.510
So I was a wet lab biologist,
pipetting and doing

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:30.980
experiments.

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:34.340
I realized pretty
quickly in my PhD--

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:37.460
I took an R class
and a Python class.

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:40.880
And then I took more CS courses.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.950
And I took more statistics
and machine learning

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:46.280
and realized pretty quickly
that was a direction that

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:49.040
would enable me to be a
better biologist and also just

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:51.930
an area of lot of
interest for me.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.470
I was lucky enough to be able
to do a ton of coursework

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:57.240
and really direct my
research that direction.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:58.850
So by the time I
finished my PhD,

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.430
I was doing fully
computational work.

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:05.000
When I finished up, I thought,
what do I want to do next?

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.888
I really enjoyed academia.

00:10:07.888 --> 00:10:08.930
I had a great time there.

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:11.850
I thought very seriously
about academic postdocs.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:14.322
And then also thought,
I really like this data

00:10:14.322 --> 00:10:15.530
side of what I've been doing.

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:18.290
I really like the statistics
and ML and all the programming

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:19.337
that I've been doing.

00:10:19.337 --> 00:10:21.420
And so I looked at data
science positions as well.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:22.880
So I applied to both.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:27.050
And in the end, I decided to
take a data science position

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:29.810
because I felt like it was
much more different than what

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:32.330
I had done before and
that I would learn more

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:35.270
in that position just because
it would really force me

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:39.080
to continue to build the skills
that I had started to develop

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:43.580
in my PhD and put me into an
entirely new problem space

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:45.505
that I hadn't been before.

00:10:45.505 --> 00:10:46.380
So that's what I did.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:49.640
I went first to a small
health insurance startup

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:50.920
called Collective Health.

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:53.043
I was there for two years.

00:10:53.043 --> 00:10:54.710
The great thing about
going to a startup

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:57.500
is you learn a lot
of data engineering.

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:01.250
And so I spent a lot of time
building pipelines and really

00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:03.230
getting to know--

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:05.390
bolstering how you
actually write code

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:08.760
in an environment
with other people

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:11.720
when your PhD is very much just
sort of you and your notebook.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:15.920
And this was now actually having
to write in a more production

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:17.160
environment.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:19.400
So I learned a lot there.

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:20.870
And I did health
insurance thinking

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:22.340
that it would be
somewhat related

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:26.820
to my PhD work, which was on
HIV and the immune system.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:31.160
It turns out not really that
much overlap between those two

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:32.677
subject areas.

00:11:32.677 --> 00:11:34.760
But I really love the data
science side of things.

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:37.310
And I really loved
having impact in business

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:40.170
and being able to see
the results of my work

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:41.450
more directly.

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:45.950
So after a couple of years I
decided to move over to Stripe.

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:52.070
Stripe was very appealing
because it was still--

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:54.110
it was big enough
to have invested

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:55.710
in data infrastructure.

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:58.250
There was a ton
of data available.

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:01.470
I was going to be able to do
more statistical and ML-type

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:01.970
work.

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:04.130
But it was still small
enough that there

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:05.810
were a lot of unsolved
problems, which

00:12:05.810 --> 00:12:09.150
is actually, five years later,
still true of Stripe today.

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:11.900
And so that's what
made me decide

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:15.890
to do-- that I was more
interested in the data problems

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:19.010
rather than focus on
a particular domain.

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:23.350
It has turned out that financial
payments are a complex system

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:25.350
just like the immune
system is a complex system.

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:28.533
So even though I do not use my
domain knowledge from my PhD

00:12:28.533 --> 00:12:30.950
that much, I still use a lot
of the skills that I learned.

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:36.210
I still use a lot of the how
to present, how to do research,

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:40.010
how to think about problems, how
to break down complex problems

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:40.770
all the time.

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:43.880
And so really have
found that if you

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:48.800
think about it the right way,
skills are pretty transferable.

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:51.050
Absolutely.

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:54.110
I'm starting to see a bunch of
other questions coming in too.

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:56.510
And Alan, I saw you
had asked one earlier.

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:58.570
Do you want to jump in/

00:12:58.570 --> 00:12:59.070
Sure.

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:00.390
Hi, Dara.

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:03.720
I'm really interested in
part of the forecasting where

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:08.655
if you have to reconcile some
SNE input from people who

00:13:08.655 --> 00:13:10.530
historically would have
been setting targets,

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:13.030
like salespeople
and their leaders.

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:15.450
And I don't know what
the organization is,

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:17.220
how it sort of maps to that.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:20.640
But what's the process
of buy in from them

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:22.650
that the forecasting
you're doing

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:25.860
seems like a realistic
enough picture of the world

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:30.450
that they buy it and that
they'll go work along it?

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:32.242
Or if their input gets
incorporated earlier

00:13:32.242 --> 00:13:33.700
in the process
somewhere so they're

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:35.790
brought along kind of from
the beginning in a way,

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:37.260
just curious how
that kind of gets

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:40.432
negotiated out in
the organization.

00:13:40.432 --> 00:13:41.640
Super interesting question.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:46.830
Yeah, and it's ongoing evolution
for how we do this interaction

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:49.470
because it's very
important to get the input

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:50.550
from stakeholders, right?

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:53.250
There might be a big sales
deal coming down the pipeline

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:55.343
that no statistical model
will know about. right?

00:13:55.343 --> 00:13:56.760
There might be
some product launch

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:59.475
plans that we're
just not going to be

00:13:59.475 --> 00:14:00.600
able to learn from history.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.330
And so we find that
interaction very important.

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:05.340
And when we started
doing this, too, when we

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:06.600
started doing the--

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.190
we call it the
statistical forecasting

00:14:08.190 --> 00:14:09.510
and the adjustments--

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:11.783
it was all done in
spreadsheets by finance folks.

00:14:11.783 --> 00:14:13.200
And we did we
really have to prove

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:17.400
our value of how does a
statistical model actually

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:18.510
help?

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:21.960
So our current system is we
have the statistical model.

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:26.190
We actually do-- that's
all done by data science.

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:30.090
We do backtesting and reports
to be able to build confidence

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:32.940
in each level of the hierarchy
so that people can understand

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:34.440
where do they expect
accuracy, where

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:36.480
do they expect less accuracy.

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:40.080
And then we have a whole process
with the finance and strategy

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:43.200
team, where they make
adjustments to the forecast

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:46.590
and are pretty clear about
where they are doing adjustments

00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:49.230
and how it's
affecting the total.

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:52.180
And then we track both, the
statistical versus the adjusted

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:52.680
forecast.

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:54.138
And what we've
learned over time is

00:14:54.138 --> 00:14:57.180
that making adjustments in
certain ways or certain places

00:14:57.180 --> 00:14:59.860
is very important
and very effective.

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:02.842
And we sort of encouraged
the finance and strategy team

00:15:02.842 --> 00:15:04.050
to focus their efforts there.

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:06.870
Whereas in other places,
the statistical model

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:11.970
is quite accurate, and it's
best to spend effort elsewhere.

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:16.460
So that's the baseline of
how we think about it--

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:18.605
again, ever evolving.

00:15:18.605 --> 00:15:20.570
But we also have an
embedded model, right?

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:22.130
So we sit with
finance and strategy.

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:24.110
We work really
closely with them.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:25.610
We really understand
their problems.

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:28.160
It's very much a
partnership-type model

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:31.400
rather than a "throw a model
over the wall" type model.

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:32.320
Great.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:33.240
That's great.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:34.620
DARA STRAUSSMAN: Did
that answer the question?

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:35.287
Yeah, it does.

00:15:35.287 --> 00:15:37.410
It's really-- and it
sounds like for having

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:40.350
been sort of driving it for
a relatively small handful

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:44.700
of years, it sounds
really mature and really

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:46.710
comprehensive in a
bunch of ways that I

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:48.150
think are really impressive.

00:15:48.150 --> 00:15:49.890
So yeah, it's cool.

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:51.737
Thank you.

00:15:51.737 --> 00:15:53.820
We still have a lot more
that we would like to do.

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:54.747
Oh, for sure, yeah.

00:15:54.747 --> 00:15:55.830
[INAUDIBLE] adjustments.

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:57.960
There's a whole literature about
adjustments and the best way

00:15:57.960 --> 00:15:58.600
to do them.

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:00.100
And we applied some
of those things.

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:01.642
But there's so much
more that I think

00:16:01.642 --> 00:16:04.150
we could do to really
maximize our effort.

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:04.830
Yeah.

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:06.790
Thank you.

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:10.540
I know you just said you sit
with the finance and strategy

00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:11.150
team.

00:16:11.150 --> 00:16:15.190
So I was curious, is there
a general data science

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:18.610
team for the whole company, or
is there data science pockets

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:19.970
within each group?

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:20.470
Yeah.

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:22.240
So GENERAL Data
science team-- and I'm

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:23.615
using sit with--
to be clear, I'm

00:16:23.615 --> 00:16:25.330
using sit with someone
figuratively here.

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:27.580
We used to sit with them
when we were fully in office.

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:29.890
And now it's sit
with them on Zoom.

00:16:29.890 --> 00:16:32.270
But centralized
data science team--

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:34.130
So we have a hybrid
embedded model.

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:37.690
So every data scientist
has a partner team

00:16:37.690 --> 00:16:39.150
that they work
really closely with.

00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:41.650
So I'm on the business side,
with the finance and operations

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:42.150
teams.

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:45.340
Others might work with
product or engineering teams.

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:47.320
And we really act like
members of that team,

00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:49.470
get to know their
problems, get to attend

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:51.220
their meetings, their
offsites, understand

00:16:51.220 --> 00:16:54.440
what's going on with them, and
do joint planning together.

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:57.040
And so we'd like to think
it's best of both worlds,

00:16:57.040 --> 00:16:59.398
making sure we are solving
real business problems.

00:16:59.398 --> 00:17:00.940
But the centralized
data science team

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:04.510
keeps us pushing the
envelope technologically and

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:06.630
innovatively as well.

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:08.730
Yeah, that's really cool.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:11.579
Deepsha, I see you had a
question over on Slido.

00:17:11.579 --> 00:17:13.790
Do you want to ask that here?

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:14.290
Sure.

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:17.200
I withdrew it because Alan's
question was slightly similar.

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:19.819
But I'll ask a
slightly different way.

00:17:19.819 --> 00:17:23.440
So you do get finance
and stakeholder buy in.

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:26.500
But sometimes when
you just recently

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:29.830
have started statistical
finance models, what

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:32.830
happens if it deflates
what the previous finance

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:34.180
predictions were?

00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:35.890
And have you had
such a scenario?

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:37.790
Because those finds
are so difficult.

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:39.770
And I have said
scenarios as well.

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:41.770
So how do you get
a buy in there?

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:43.630
Yeah.

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:45.047
It's a great question.

00:17:45.047 --> 00:17:46.630
As much as you can
bring in confidence

00:17:46.630 --> 00:17:49.780
intervals or backtesting
statistics and show here's

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:52.780
what we expect,
those, of course,

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:54.850
are only as good
as your input data.

00:17:54.850 --> 00:17:57.308
And that can be challenging
when you're first starting out.

00:17:57.308 --> 00:18:00.070
But that is-- finance folks
are very quantitative, right?

00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:01.220
They're very data oriented.

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:02.440
They want to know how.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:05.470
At least our finance books are.

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:07.768
So that helps.

00:18:07.768 --> 00:18:10.310
In our experience, it did take
some time just to build trust.

00:18:10.310 --> 00:18:13.480
And so it was making sure that
we had all the very detailed

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:14.900
tracking in place.

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:16.420
And so it wasn't
saying, hey, we're

00:18:16.420 --> 00:18:17.918
going to replace your model.

00:18:17.918 --> 00:18:19.960
It was saying, let's run
them in parallel, right?

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:23.620
And let's just track the
statistical model, the finance

00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:25.300
model, maybe some
combination of them

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:27.100
if we want to do
an adjusted model.

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:32.710
And let's look for six months
at how those go and see.

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:35.020
Let's just sort of be
objective about it, if we can.

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:38.500
And that ultimately
was very helpful for us

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:40.270
in building some
trust and confidence.

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:41.980
But it is a relationship.

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:44.558
It does take some time to build.

00:18:44.558 --> 00:18:45.100
Definitely.

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:48.160
Thanks, Dara.

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:50.160
Thank you all for all
the great questions too.

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:54.450
I see one on Slido was,
with your forecasting,

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:57.510
how do you share results to
those who make decisions?

00:18:57.510 --> 00:18:59.880
Is it through Excel, PowerPoint?

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:03.593
Do you ever use Shiny
or SharePoint, Power BI?

00:19:03.593 --> 00:19:05.010
I would love it
if we had Shiny.

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:06.720
We don't have it
set up at Stripe.

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:11.040
So we have our own internal
role forecasting-- sorry, not

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:14.490
forecasting, dashboarding
tool that is SQL based

00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:18.900
and allows us to build
pretty nice dashboards

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:20.620
that way on our data.

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:23.760
So we use a lot of that.

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:27.750
We will sometimes make
sort of bespoke plots

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:30.600
in our R or Python
and share those.

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:34.380
We do a lot of PowerPoint or
Google Slides presentations

00:19:34.380 --> 00:19:34.890
as well.

00:19:37.610 --> 00:19:39.880
And sometimes when we're
working directly with

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:43.480
there certainly are sort of
queries and spreadsheets.

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:46.600
And we try to speak their
language where we can.

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:49.095
So while we might operate
in more of a SQL land,

00:19:49.095 --> 00:19:51.220
they might be operating
more of a spreadsheet land.

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:55.380
And so we try to interface
between those things.

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:55.880
Great.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:57.710
Thank you.

00:19:57.710 --> 00:20:00.872
And Abigail, I love your
question in the chat.

00:20:00.872 --> 00:20:03.080
I know we get to hear a lot
of different perspectives

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:04.430
on this one.

00:20:04.430 --> 00:20:06.290
And I'd love to have
you ask that here.

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:10.610
Oh.

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:12.110
I don't know if we
can hear you yet.

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:12.950
So I can ask it.

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:15.470
And feel free to jump
in after if you want.

00:20:15.470 --> 00:20:17.300
But the question
was, how do you feel

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:20.660
about being a manager
versus being in a purely SME

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:22.430
individual contributor role?

00:20:22.430 --> 00:20:25.820
Do you still get to code?

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:27.170
These days not as much.

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:28.940
I started as an IC at stripe.

00:20:28.940 --> 00:20:32.120
And I transitioned about
a year into my tenure.

00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:34.460
And when I first transitioned,
managing a small team,

00:20:34.460 --> 00:20:36.800
I still did about 50% coding.

00:20:36.800 --> 00:20:40.130
And now I manage usually
between 8 to 10 folks.

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:43.340
So I do more pure
management stuff.

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:45.770
I think I've really
enjoyed the transition.

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:48.830
I find that I think
the key question

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:50.690
to ask yourself if
you're thinking about IC

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:53.300
versus management
is, how motivated you

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:58.910
will be by enabling other folks
to do work versus how motivated

00:20:58.910 --> 00:21:03.170
are you by solving problems
and shipping work yourself?

00:21:03.170 --> 00:21:05.480
And most people
would be motivated

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:06.510
by both of those things.

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:08.343
But it's really sort
of trying to figure out

00:21:08.343 --> 00:21:11.750
for yourself where you want to
prioritize spending your time

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:14.900
and how helpful
each of those roles

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:17.900
might be to you in
achieving that goal.

00:21:17.900 --> 00:21:19.010
That's my short answer.

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:20.390
I'm happy to go on more.

00:21:20.390 --> 00:21:22.650
These are great questions.

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:25.270
Thank you.

00:21:25.270 --> 00:21:27.792
Eugene, I see you had
a question in the chat.

00:21:27.792 --> 00:21:28.750
Do you to jump in next?

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:35.840
EUGENE GEIS: Sure.

00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:39.560
My question is just about the
sophistication of modeling.

00:21:39.560 --> 00:21:44.510
So you seem to discuss quite a
few rather sophisticated models

00:21:44.510 --> 00:21:48.710
in your forecasting as you
gave your short summary of what

00:21:48.710 --> 00:21:49.670
you're using.

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:53.420
I'm just curious,
has the increase in--

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:55.010
I assume you started
simple, right?

00:21:55.010 --> 00:21:57.860
So has the increase
in sophistication

00:21:57.860 --> 00:22:04.970
actually significantly changed
your results or your outcomes

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:07.820
as the models have evolved?

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:08.320
Yeah.

00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:12.210
This is-- this is
a good question.

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:15.950
So our forecast, I
think the way that we

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:18.230
do the hierarchical
reconciliation

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:19.340
is very important.

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:23.390
And that has certainly increased
in sophistication over time.

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:25.610
Underlying models
themselves, we actually

00:22:25.610 --> 00:22:28.130
don't use anything--
most of the time,

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:30.530
they're not super fancy
forecasting models.

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:33.170
We're not doing some
of the things that

00:22:33.170 --> 00:22:36.320
are winning the M5 forecasting
competition these days.

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:39.320
It's sort of bread
and butter ARIMA

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:42.460
and exponential smoothing
for the most part.

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:44.660
We'll play with
other models too.

00:22:44.660 --> 00:22:47.900
And we have found that for our
particular business problem,

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:50.090
that works really
well because we have

00:22:50.090 --> 00:22:51.530
a decent amount of history.

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:54.350
We have-- very important
to capture seasonality.

00:22:54.350 --> 00:22:55.760
We're doing monthly forecasts.

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:59.940
So we are able to capture what
we need to capture for those

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:03.290
So the choice of
forecasting model

00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:09.740
is so dependent on the problem
that you have and the--

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:12.330
there are so many things to
consider about your input data.

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:16.740
I think if we had a lot
of external aggressors

00:23:16.740 --> 00:23:20.400
that we were trying to model
a lot of parallel processes

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:23.110
happening, then,
probably, different kinds

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:25.110
of models, potentially
more of the deep learning

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:27.240
forecasting models that
have come into play

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:30.280
recently might be really
helpful to us as well.

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:33.990
But for the most part, a lot
of bread-and-butter forecasting

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:35.040
models work well for us.

00:23:38.605 --> 00:23:39.105
Thank you.

00:23:39.105 --> 00:23:42.120
And Trevor, I know you had
a somewhat similar question.

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:44.660
So I just wanted to see if that
addressed your question too,

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:46.440
or if you want to
add on to that.

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:47.180
TREVOR: Yeah.

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:48.260
Hi Rachael.

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:50.330
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: M Hi, Trevor.

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:54.980
TREVOR: It is indeed a
connected question because--

00:23:54.980 --> 00:24:00.440
so most forecasting models
look at historical data

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:02.150
to predict the future, right?

00:24:02.150 --> 00:24:05.780
So that is assuming that the
past is the future, right?

00:24:05.780 --> 00:24:08.660
And of course, we all understand
that can change, right?

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:10.880
So for example, when
COVID came along,

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:12.500
it disrupted many models, right?

00:24:12.500 --> 00:24:15.710
Nobody could have
forecasted that COVID

00:24:15.710 --> 00:24:19.310
would have had the effect that
it had on businesses, right?

00:24:19.310 --> 00:24:27.550
So I'm just wondering to what
extent, Dara, there are factors

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:30.250
that would not have
been in your models that

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:33.700
would have affected maybe
the accuracy of your models?

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:34.360
That's one.

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:39.250
And, two, we use historical
data to make predictions.

00:24:39.250 --> 00:24:42.640
At the end of the day--

00:24:42.640 --> 00:24:44.500
let's say your
forecasting sales, right?

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:48.700
And you have forecast it to
be a certain amount based

00:24:48.700 --> 00:24:49.810
on historical data.

00:24:49.810 --> 00:24:53.050
But then when reality happens,
at the end of a period

00:24:53.050 --> 00:24:54.740
you get the actual numbers.

00:24:54.740 --> 00:24:57.580
So I want to know
two things really.

00:24:57.580 --> 00:25:01.660
How close are you
typically to your forecast?

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:06.010
And how do you manage factors
that weren't considered

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:09.595
in the model in the first
place because of you

00:25:09.595 --> 00:25:11.380
weren't thinking about those?

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:13.370
I hope I'm clear.

00:25:13.370 --> 00:25:14.350
Yeah.

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:16.720
Some fundamental
problems of forecasting

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:18.850
in a business setting.

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:22.570
We do include external
aggressors and--

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:23.350
now.

00:25:23.350 --> 00:25:26.020
Of course, we didn't
predict COVID.

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:27.850
When I look back
now, forecasting

00:25:27.850 --> 00:25:29.110
was so easy pre-COVID.

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:32.360
And now, post-COVID, it's a
whole different ball game.

00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:34.480
So now we do include
aggressors that

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:40.090
try to account for the past
COVID changes in our data.

00:25:40.090 --> 00:25:42.310
What we don't want is
to continue forecasting

00:25:42.310 --> 00:25:44.300
more COVIDs in the future.

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:46.690
So we do have some
sort of external things

00:25:46.690 --> 00:25:49.450
that we use that help
us to predict that.

00:25:49.450 --> 00:25:53.020
We also do factor in
macroeconomic variables

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:56.620
on top of our statistical
forecasts that--

00:25:56.620 --> 00:25:59.230
again, you still have to
make your own predictions

00:25:59.230 --> 00:26:02.260
and sort of decide as a company
what you think might happen.

00:26:02.260 --> 00:26:04.360
We do sometimes do
scenario-based planning

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:06.280
as well, baseline,
and here's sort

00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:08.230
of the upside or the
downside scenario

00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:10.690
to try to give the business
some framework for how

00:26:10.690 --> 00:26:13.780
to think about possibilities.

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:16.450
Accuracy and what do we do
in the forecast is off--

00:26:16.450 --> 00:26:18.610
so with the
hierarchical forecast,

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:20.410
the general pattern
is that we are not

00:26:20.410 --> 00:26:23.020
that accurate at the lowest
levels of the hierarchy,

00:26:23.020 --> 00:26:23.830
generally.

00:26:23.830 --> 00:26:29.980
If we're forecasting one cohort,
one region, one business type,

00:26:29.980 --> 00:26:32.020
that gets to be a pretty
small set of data.

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:34.318
We don't have as much
history, or there

00:26:34.318 --> 00:26:35.860
may be erratic
patterns in it, and so

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:38.200
we don't have as much
accuracy down there.

00:26:38.200 --> 00:26:41.060
But then when you
add up to the total,

00:26:41.060 --> 00:26:43.420
you have a much more
robust data set.

00:26:43.420 --> 00:26:46.150
And we tend to be more
accurate at the higher

00:26:46.150 --> 00:26:48.100
levels of the forecast.

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:49.120
We do a lot of tracking.

00:26:49.120 --> 00:26:50.500
We create a lot of dashboards.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:53.650
We try to be really clear
with where we're doing well

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:54.940
and where we're not.

00:26:54.940 --> 00:26:56.680
We do reforecast
throughout the year

00:26:56.680 --> 00:27:03.150
to be able to update our
best guess on where we think

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:05.130
we're going to land,
given more data has

00:27:05.130 --> 00:27:07.200
come in throughout the year.

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:09.182
So that is helpful.

00:27:09.182 --> 00:27:11.640
And then we just sit side by
side with finance and strategy

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:14.070
and do the analysis
and try to understand

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:15.480
when the forecast was off?

00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:17.310
What are the segments
in which it was off?

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:18.990
Can we identify the
root cause for that?

00:27:18.990 --> 00:27:21.180
And how can we improve
our forecast model

00:27:21.180 --> 00:27:22.720
for the next iteration?

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:25.620
So no magic bullet, certainly.

00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:31.010
But a lot of continual efforts
to try to improve over time.

00:27:31.010 --> 00:27:32.850
TREVOR: Thank you.

00:27:32.850 --> 00:27:34.770
Thank you very much.

