00:33:37.280 --> 00:33:38.280
Similar background here.

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:41.370
I'm a data science professional
working in a finance company.

00:33:41.370 --> 00:33:44.970
And before that, you worked
in computational immunology.

00:33:44.970 --> 00:33:46.650
And for me, I worked
in biochemistry.

00:33:46.650 --> 00:33:48.820
So I'm a similar pathway here.

00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:50.080
So I have one question.

00:33:50.080 --> 00:33:53.550
So because we both know
that for forecasting models

00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:56.340
in the finance field,
one of the challenges

00:33:56.340 --> 00:33:59.280
is to incorporate
macroeconomic factors,

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:04.450
such as GDP, unemployment rate,
inflation, all these things.

00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:06.540
So I'm wondering for
all the forecasting

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:09.690
models you mentioned,
do either of them

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:11.340
use macroeconomic variables?

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:14.040
And if it does,
what do you think

00:34:14.040 --> 00:34:18.120
is the best practice to
incorporate such variables?

00:34:18.120 --> 00:34:19.540
Yep, good question.

00:34:19.540 --> 00:34:20.040
Yep.

00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:24.635
This is very important to us
and with 2022, for example,

00:34:24.635 --> 00:34:26.010
became even more
important for us

00:34:26.010 --> 00:34:30.909
to understand the effect of the
macro economy on our forecast.

00:34:30.909 --> 00:34:31.500
So we do.

00:34:31.500 --> 00:34:36.630
We currently have this as sort
of a second layer of modeling

00:34:36.630 --> 00:34:40.679
that's sort of an
overlay that we

00:34:40.679 --> 00:34:43.440
put on our statistical
model, where we say--

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:46.170
we do our best to learn as
much as we can from the past

00:34:46.170 --> 00:34:49.739
about how things like GDP
might affect our forecast

00:34:49.739 --> 00:34:55.272
and apply those as a
secondary model to our model.

00:34:55.272 --> 00:34:56.730
One thing that's
worked really well

00:34:56.730 --> 00:35:02.870
is having scenarios here
because we can make our best

00:35:02.870 --> 00:35:04.910
predictions, but
in the end, it's

00:35:04.910 --> 00:35:07.708
still predictions with these
macroeconomic factors, right?

00:35:07.708 --> 00:35:09.500
It's not as if
incorporating them magically

00:35:09.500 --> 00:35:10.542
makes your forecast work.

00:35:10.542 --> 00:35:13.518
You still have to
decide what the future's

00:35:13.518 --> 00:35:14.310
going to look like.

00:35:14.310 --> 00:35:18.350
And so having different
scenarios, high, medium, low,

00:35:18.350 --> 00:35:20.090
or however we set it
up different ways,

00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:23.390
different cycles, to give the
business some indication of,

00:35:23.390 --> 00:35:25.070
we can't predict
the future either.

00:35:25.070 --> 00:35:27.570
We don't know what's going to
happen with the macro economy.

00:35:27.570 --> 00:35:30.800
But this at least
allows them to say, OK,

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:35.000
if something goes well, here's
what we might expect to happen.

00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:39.140
If we want to plan to a
sort of worst-case scenario,

00:35:39.140 --> 00:35:40.640
here's how we might do that.

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:43.730
And you can imagine
different sort

00:35:43.730 --> 00:35:45.372
of business
decision-making scenarios

00:35:45.372 --> 00:35:46.955
they might want to
use different ones.

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:51.480
Thank you.

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:53.460
And Marlene, I see
your hand's raised too.

00:35:57.430 --> 00:35:57.972
[INAUDIBLE]

00:35:57.972 --> 00:35:59.222
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: There you go.

00:35:59.222 --> 00:36:00.020
We can hear you.

00:36:00.020 --> 00:36:02.390
Yeah.

00:36:02.390 --> 00:36:03.877
So hi.

00:36:03.877 --> 00:36:05.460
Long time caller,
first time listener.

00:36:05.460 --> 00:36:06.335
No, I'm just kidding.

00:36:06.335 --> 00:36:09.090
Dara, thanks for
presenting this morning.

00:36:09.090 --> 00:36:09.590
Sorry.

00:36:09.590 --> 00:36:11.007
The coffee's just
kicked in, gang.

00:36:11.007 --> 00:36:12.590
So I'm all over the place.

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:17.210
I've shared on
numerous calls before

00:36:17.210 --> 00:36:19.790
that I'm just kind of an
army of one in my company.

00:36:19.790 --> 00:36:22.430
We don't have a formal
data science team.

00:36:22.430 --> 00:36:27.560
We're far from the machine
learning area of tech.

00:36:27.560 --> 00:36:30.530
And right now my
focus is just building

00:36:30.530 --> 00:36:33.800
a culture that understands that
there's a need for data science

00:36:33.800 --> 00:36:37.100
and giving some practical
uses for automation

00:36:37.100 --> 00:36:40.400
and better analytics.

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:42.680
So my question to you is--

00:36:42.680 --> 00:36:44.270
it's more around mentorship.

00:36:44.270 --> 00:36:48.710
I wanted to know if you guys
had any kind of culture that

00:36:48.710 --> 00:36:50.630
promotes, say, mentorship?

00:36:50.630 --> 00:36:55.070
Do you, say, have a
senior data scientist

00:36:55.070 --> 00:36:58.580
that takes a junior under their
wing, or even a fledgling data

00:36:58.580 --> 00:37:01.940
scientist, just somebody who's
got some great analytic prowess

00:37:01.940 --> 00:37:04.790
that somehow gets-- they'll
coming into the fold?

00:37:04.790 --> 00:37:05.740
I don't know.

00:37:05.740 --> 00:37:08.430
Take that question
where you want to go.

00:37:08.430 --> 00:37:10.580
But it's around
mentorship, essentially.

00:37:10.580 --> 00:37:11.473
Thanks, Dara.

00:37:11.473 --> 00:37:12.140
Totally, yeah.

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:13.477
Thank you for asking.

00:37:13.477 --> 00:37:14.810
We do have a mentorship program.

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:19.040
So we have-- there is
one within data science.

00:37:19.040 --> 00:37:21.860
Our team is-- what
are we around--

00:37:21.860 --> 00:37:24.840
170, 180 folks right now.

00:37:24.840 --> 00:37:27.950
So we've gotten
big enough that we

00:37:27.950 --> 00:37:31.280
do have a spectrum of more
junior to more senior data

00:37:31.280 --> 00:37:32.450
scientists.

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:38.840
We do pair them up and allow
them to exchange knowledge

00:37:38.840 --> 00:37:40.160
and mentorship.

00:37:40.160 --> 00:37:42.440
More formally, we have--

00:37:42.440 --> 00:37:44.600
some years we have an
intern program, as well,

00:37:44.600 --> 00:37:48.350
where we've taken in folks
who are sort of finishing up

00:37:48.350 --> 00:37:52.160
some level of schooling and
had them as summer interns

00:37:52.160 --> 00:37:54.085
on data science.

00:37:54.085 --> 00:37:55.460
A lot of them have
converted then

00:37:55.460 --> 00:37:57.960
to full-time data scientists,
and that has worked out really

00:37:57.960 --> 00:37:58.738
well for us.

00:37:58.738 --> 00:38:00.530
We also have a very
strong learning culture

00:38:00.530 --> 00:38:01.700
on our data science team.

00:38:01.700 --> 00:38:05.390
And so we-- part of having a
centralized data science team

00:38:05.390 --> 00:38:06.450
brings this benefit.

00:38:06.450 --> 00:38:08.838
So we do a lot of
lunch and learns,

00:38:08.838 --> 00:38:10.880
where we teach each other
about our current work.

00:38:10.880 --> 00:38:13.675
We have a learning group,
where we will read a blog post.

00:38:13.675 --> 00:38:15.050
Or at some points,
we've actually

00:38:15.050 --> 00:38:17.390
gone through a whole
Coursera course together.

00:38:17.390 --> 00:38:19.970
Or depending on the
interests of the group,

00:38:19.970 --> 00:38:24.360
we'll sort of work
together on those.

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:27.350
And then we have various sort
of targeted seminars at Stripe,

00:38:27.350 --> 00:38:27.990
too, as well.

00:38:27.990 --> 00:38:29.360
So there's the ML seminar.

00:38:29.360 --> 00:38:31.760
There's a more
general tech seminar.

00:38:31.760 --> 00:38:34.590
Sometimes we'll bring in
external speakers as well.

00:38:34.590 --> 00:38:37.430
So I think that can be--

00:38:37.430 --> 00:38:39.080
that's probably not
unique to Stripe.

00:38:39.080 --> 00:38:41.122
I think that's something
that data science brings

00:38:41.122 --> 00:38:43.820
to a company really well is most
data scientists are motivated

00:38:43.820 --> 00:38:46.100
by continual
learning, continuing

00:38:46.100 --> 00:38:48.470
to expand your skills, getting
to try out new methods,

00:38:48.470 --> 00:38:54.890
getting to just continue
to both add both depth

00:38:54.890 --> 00:38:57.840
and breadth to your
technical skill set.

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:02.058
So, yeah, I found that to be a
really positive part of working

00:39:02.058 --> 00:39:03.350
as part of a data science team.

00:39:06.470 --> 00:39:08.220
And I see Adrian
had a follow up there

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:12.090
on which Coursera course
did the team do together?

00:39:12.090 --> 00:39:14.220
This was, like, two
or three years ago.

00:39:14.220 --> 00:39:15.620
We did the deep learning course.

00:39:15.620 --> 00:39:17.870
At the time, there was just,
I think, one general deep

00:39:17.870 --> 00:39:18.537
learning course.

00:39:18.537 --> 00:39:21.890
It probably has expanded now.

00:39:21.890 --> 00:39:22.390
Nice.

00:39:22.390 --> 00:39:25.510
And kind of, I guess, follow-up
question too Marlene's,

00:39:25.510 --> 00:39:28.180
too, I was poking
around on LinkedIn

00:39:28.180 --> 00:39:30.670
and saw a few roles at Stripe.

00:39:30.670 --> 00:39:33.190
And I noticed that
one of them mentioned

00:39:33.190 --> 00:39:36.350
helping to build the data
science community there.

00:39:36.350 --> 00:39:38.980
And so aside from
some of the learning

00:39:38.980 --> 00:39:41.350
and getting together
to learn together,

00:39:41.350 --> 00:39:44.650
are there other ways that
people highlight their work,

00:39:44.650 --> 00:39:47.710
like internal meetup
groups or things like that

00:39:47.710 --> 00:39:49.850
or Slack or Teams channels?

00:39:49.850 --> 00:39:50.350
Yeah.

00:39:50.350 --> 00:39:56.820
We have-- so we have a very
big written culture at Stripe.

00:39:56.820 --> 00:39:58.050
And that includes emails.

00:39:58.050 --> 00:40:01.230
And so we send what we call
shipped emails company wide.

00:40:01.230 --> 00:40:02.640
So you finish a project.

00:40:02.640 --> 00:40:05.768
You're encouraged to write
it up and send it to--

00:40:05.768 --> 00:40:08.310
if it's a very big project, you
send it to the whole company.

00:40:08.310 --> 00:40:09.780
If it's sort of a
medium-sized project,

00:40:09.780 --> 00:40:12.155
we might send it to the data
science team and our partner

00:40:12.155 --> 00:40:12.960
teams.

00:40:12.960 --> 00:40:16.830
But strong culture of shipping
and celebrating work that way,

00:40:16.830 --> 00:40:18.810
as well as making
sure that your work

00:40:18.810 --> 00:40:20.550
has as much impact as possible.

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:24.660
Sometimes sharing it with
another team or another person

00:40:24.660 --> 00:40:27.180
actually helps you find
another use case for something

00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:32.070
that you've built. So strong
culture of sharing there.

00:40:32.070 --> 00:40:34.570
We have various levels
of team meetings as well.

00:40:34.570 --> 00:40:36.480
So whole data
science team meeting

00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:39.990
where we do sort of lightning
talks, a few short talks.

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:42.630
And so that's a big
group about recent work.

00:40:42.630 --> 00:40:46.065
And then we have our
medium meeting, where--

00:40:46.065 --> 00:40:49.830
medium meeting, like,
maybe 30 or 40 people,

00:40:49.830 --> 00:40:53.070
where we give maybe a
15-minute presentation

00:40:53.070 --> 00:40:54.240
on more polished work.

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:56.250
And then in my team
meeting of 10 people,

00:40:56.250 --> 00:40:58.890
we do very early stage
technical feedback.

00:40:58.890 --> 00:41:01.890
And so for that one, we
require that you do not

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:04.890
prepare anything for it
and just put up your code,

00:41:04.890 --> 00:41:08.430
put up your doc, put up your
random notes on something,

00:41:08.430 --> 00:41:11.190
and talk through
something very early stage

00:41:11.190 --> 00:41:12.360
that you're thinking about.

00:41:12.360 --> 00:41:13.902
And we found that
one, in particular,

00:41:13.902 --> 00:41:15.960
to be really helpful
to folks, this sort

00:41:15.960 --> 00:41:21.000
of low-stakes environment where
you can bounce your ideas off

00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:22.180
of each other.

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:23.610
Especially in
COVID times, that's

00:41:23.610 --> 00:41:26.790
been really helpful to
building some team camaraderie

00:41:26.790 --> 00:41:28.485
and also sharing good feedback.

00:41:33.538 --> 00:41:35.580
Alan, I see you just
put something in the chat.

00:41:35.580 --> 00:41:38.040
You want to jump in next?

00:41:38.040 --> 00:41:39.180
Sure.

00:41:39.180 --> 00:41:42.310
I'm thinking of lots
of questions today.

00:41:42.310 --> 00:41:45.720
I'm curious, Dara, if you have
a balance that you can maintain

00:41:45.720 --> 00:41:47.730
between the sort
of routine things

00:41:47.730 --> 00:41:49.770
like financial
forecasting that has

00:41:49.770 --> 00:41:52.890
an annual cadence or a quarterly
requirement or whatever

00:41:52.890 --> 00:41:58.020
with room to experiment
and learn new methods,

00:41:58.020 --> 00:42:01.990
try out tools, shop those
around into the organization?

00:42:01.990 --> 00:42:05.520
And how do you meet all
those needed commitments

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:09.810
with-- how do you balance
that with room to explore,

00:42:09.810 --> 00:42:14.635
room to grow for your team
to make sure that they're

00:42:14.635 --> 00:42:17.010
able to try things out that
may not have a known business

00:42:17.010 --> 00:42:20.700
connection yet, but
you want to give them

00:42:20.700 --> 00:42:22.140
that kind of opportunity?

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:23.640
Is that something
you're able to do?

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:26.550
And if so, how do you do it?

00:42:26.550 --> 00:42:28.390
Yeah, million-dollar question.

00:42:28.390 --> 00:42:31.060
So we do a few strategies here.

00:42:31.060 --> 00:42:33.120
This also is something
that we continually

00:42:33.120 --> 00:42:35.400
work on and try to promote.

00:42:35.400 --> 00:42:40.900
We do planning each
half with our partners.

00:42:40.900 --> 00:42:43.530
And so we aim to
get-- and when we

00:42:43.530 --> 00:42:45.420
do this sort of
biannual planning,

00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:48.870
we aim to get the most important
projects to them on our roadmap

00:42:48.870 --> 00:42:52.290
so that we can jointly
decide what those are.

00:42:52.290 --> 00:42:54.288
And that allows--
typically, there

00:42:54.288 --> 00:42:55.830
within those larger
projects, there's

00:42:55.830 --> 00:43:00.240
some room for new methods or
innovation, experimentation,

00:43:00.240 --> 00:43:02.130
while still working
towards something

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:05.130
that is key to the business
and that partners have decided

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:06.850
is important to them.

00:43:06.850 --> 00:43:09.300
And so that works
well for trying

00:43:09.300 --> 00:43:13.470
to build in a bit more of that
sort of longer-term project

00:43:13.470 --> 00:43:16.530
that allows the data scientists
to dig in on the methodology

00:43:16.530 --> 00:43:17.610
side.

00:43:17.610 --> 00:43:20.530
But then, of course, we build
flexibility into the process

00:43:20.530 --> 00:43:21.030
too.

00:43:21.030 --> 00:43:22.080
Things come up.

00:43:22.080 --> 00:43:24.720
There may be urgent things--
there are all the time-- that

00:43:24.720 --> 00:43:29.380
come up that are very important
as well that folks work on.

00:43:29.380 --> 00:43:33.210
And so we just sort of
continually evaluate those.

00:43:33.210 --> 00:43:35.778
And I spend a ton of time
just having conversations

00:43:35.778 --> 00:43:37.320
about resourcing
and making tradeoffs

00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:39.570
on what the team is working on.

00:43:39.570 --> 00:43:42.480
I see that as a key
role of a manager.

00:43:42.480 --> 00:43:45.450
We also do-- we have a good
tradition of quarterly career

00:43:45.450 --> 00:43:46.260
chats.

00:43:46.260 --> 00:43:47.910
So I do quarterly
chats with everyone

00:43:47.910 --> 00:43:50.640
on my team, talk about their--

00:43:50.640 --> 00:43:52.483
chance to zoom out
and talk longer term

00:43:52.483 --> 00:43:53.400
about their interests.

00:43:53.400 --> 00:43:56.005
And from there I find
out what kind of projects

00:43:56.005 --> 00:43:58.380
are they interested in that
they're not currently working

00:43:58.620 --> 00:44:00.745
Or are there things they're
working on that they're

00:44:00.745 --> 00:44:02.978
less interested in?

00:44:02.978 --> 00:44:04.770
You have to find the
business needs, right?

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:05.920
We're not a research team.

00:44:05.920 --> 00:44:09.930
We don't do sort
of pure R&amp;amp;D work.

00:44:09.930 --> 00:44:12.270
But often by just
having a good grasp

00:44:12.270 --> 00:44:14.130
on where people want
to go and what they're

00:44:14.130 --> 00:44:16.110
interested in-- when I
hear about new projects,

00:44:16.110 --> 00:44:20.990
I'm able to help target
those appropriately to folks.

00:44:20.990 --> 00:44:21.560
Awesome.

00:44:21.560 --> 00:44:22.060
Thanks.

00:44:22.060 --> 00:44:23.935
A lot of that sounds
familiar, stuff that I'm

00:44:23.935 --> 00:44:27.388
trying to do better as well.

00:44:27.388 --> 00:44:28.262
Yeah.

00:44:28.262 --> 00:44:29.720
It's a continual
process, for sure.

00:44:29.720 --> 00:44:30.678
ALAN SCHUSSMAN: Uh-huh.

00:44:30.678 --> 00:44:32.200
Yeah.

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:34.880
I was just making a note of
the quarterly career charts

00:44:34.880 --> 00:44:35.380
too.

00:44:35.380 --> 00:44:37.990
And I was just curious
how do you frame

00:44:37.990 --> 00:44:40.360
that with your direct reports?

00:44:40.360 --> 00:44:42.880
Is that an actual
formal process that they

00:44:42.880 --> 00:44:45.680
go through or just
informal conversation?

00:44:45.680 --> 00:44:48.160
So the first time when I have
a new direct report, either

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:51.610
new to Stripe or new to
reporting to me, I ask them--

00:44:51.610 --> 00:44:54.310
it's a fairly-- a
more structured chat.

00:44:54.310 --> 00:44:55.810
We go through their
career history

00:44:55.810 --> 00:44:57.772
and ask a lot of questions
about the decisions

00:44:57.772 --> 00:44:59.980
that they've made along
their way and their interests

00:44:59.980 --> 00:45:02.740
and what brought them
to Stripe and a lot

00:45:02.740 --> 00:45:06.730
of just getting to understand
their career motivations.

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:10.910
For subsequent ones,
it is more open.

00:45:10.910 --> 00:45:13.690
A lot of folks have a
more structured idea

00:45:13.690 --> 00:45:15.190
of what they want
from their career.

00:45:15.190 --> 00:45:17.920
And we might talk through
that plan each time.

00:45:17.920 --> 00:45:20.920
I'll often come with
questions or new frameworks

00:45:20.920 --> 00:45:23.650
that I'm thinking about for
how to think about your career

00:45:23.650 --> 00:45:25.360
and bring those.

00:45:25.360 --> 00:45:29.140
Some folks are either earlier
or more open or less sure

00:45:29.140 --> 00:45:30.130
about their career.

00:45:30.130 --> 00:45:32.780
And in that case, I might direct
the conversation a little bit

00:45:32.780 --> 00:45:33.280
more.

00:45:33.280 --> 00:45:38.010
So it's fairly tailored
to the individual.

00:45:38.010 --> 00:45:39.540
Thank you.

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:41.670
So I see two anonymous
questions that

00:45:41.670 --> 00:45:43.120
are kind of on the same topic.

00:45:43.120 --> 00:45:45.780
So let me combine
them both here.

00:45:45.780 --> 00:45:49.980
But the questions are around,
do you support both R and Python

00:45:49.980 --> 00:45:51.180
users on your team?

00:45:51.180 --> 00:45:54.750
And what does that
split look like?

00:45:54.750 --> 00:45:56.620
Actually, let me
ask that part first

00:45:56.620 --> 00:45:59.370
so I don't throw too
many questions at once.

00:45:59.370 --> 00:46:00.060
We do.

00:46:00.060 --> 00:46:00.600
We do.

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:04.970
We used to do all of our
forecasting R actually.

00:46:04.970 --> 00:46:07.590
And recently, Python
has stepped it up a bit.

00:46:07.590 --> 00:46:10.222
And we've been doing
a lot more in Python.

00:46:10.222 --> 00:46:12.680
Part of the reason for that is
that Stripe is somewhat more

00:46:12.680 --> 00:46:15.180
Python based in
our infrastructure.

00:46:15.180 --> 00:46:17.450
And so doing it in
Python allows us

00:46:17.450 --> 00:46:20.090
to integrate with the
rest of Stripe systems

00:46:20.090 --> 00:46:21.950
much more easily.

00:46:21.950 --> 00:46:24.020
We do support R here.

00:46:24.020 --> 00:46:25.340
We have R notebooks.

00:46:25.340 --> 00:46:29.450
We have the ability
to schedule jobs in R.

00:46:29.450 --> 00:46:33.410
But I'd say it's more like 20%
to 30% of data science usage.

00:46:33.410 --> 00:46:37.147
And Python is a little bit more,
which R is close to my heart.

00:46:37.147 --> 00:46:39.230
That's the programming
language that I started out

00:46:39.230 --> 00:46:40.100
in grad school.

00:46:40.100 --> 00:46:42.590
And I could live in
the tidyverse all day.

00:46:42.590 --> 00:46:45.320
But we use it a
bit more for sort

00:46:45.320 --> 00:46:48.710
of analytical, exploratory
analysis work, I would say,

00:46:48.710 --> 00:46:53.940
and Python a bit more
for in production jobs.

00:46:53.940 --> 00:46:56.200
In the follow-up
question, there was,

00:46:56.200 --> 00:46:59.190
do you encourage and support
folks who want to learn one

00:46:59.190 --> 00:47:01.980
while being more
proficient in the other?

00:47:01.980 --> 00:47:02.730
Yeah, certainly.

00:47:02.730 --> 00:47:05.910
I just had someone on my team
who was more proficient in R

00:47:05.910 --> 00:47:07.382
but wanted to learn more Python.

00:47:07.382 --> 00:47:09.090
And we sort of designed
a project for her

00:47:09.090 --> 00:47:11.520
that allowed her to do that.

00:47:11.520 --> 00:47:13.470
Awesome.

00:47:13.470 --> 00:47:19.790
Another anonymous question from
earlier was, based on the fact

00:47:19.790 --> 00:47:22.490
that there would be underlying
uncertainties in the forecast,

00:47:22.490 --> 00:47:25.220
how do you make your
partner teams aware of

00:47:25.220 --> 00:47:27.560
and consider those
uncertainties?

00:47:30.110 --> 00:47:33.850
Yeah, a complex question.

00:47:33.850 --> 00:47:35.290
We talk about it
a lot with them.

00:47:35.290 --> 00:47:36.630
We show the backtesting results.

00:47:36.630 --> 00:47:39.828
Those are usually helpful
as sort of quantitative--

00:47:39.828 --> 00:47:41.370
maybe it doesn't
tell you everything.

00:47:41.370 --> 00:47:44.580
But it tells you
something about when

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:47.070
we looked over the past year,
here's the amount of error

00:47:47.070 --> 00:47:47.760
that we saw.

00:47:47.760 --> 00:47:49.927
Here's how you might think
about that going forward.

00:47:52.230 --> 00:47:55.500
There's a lot of fancier
joint uncertainty

00:47:55.500 --> 00:47:57.690
throughout the
hierarchy methodologies

00:47:57.690 --> 00:47:59.670
that we are exploring as well.

00:47:59.670 --> 00:48:03.480
I think uncertainty is one
of the most useful concepts

00:48:03.480 --> 00:48:06.060
for forecasting
within the business.

00:48:06.060 --> 00:48:12.030
But it's very hard to propagate
the right level of uncertainty

00:48:12.030 --> 00:48:15.870
throughout the hierarchy because
you introduce uncertainty

00:48:15.870 --> 00:48:18.690
both at the modeling,
the initial modeling,

00:48:18.690 --> 00:48:21.150
various points, and
in the reconciliation.

00:48:21.150 --> 00:48:28.440
And so that's somewhat more
of an interesting problem

00:48:28.440 --> 00:48:32.400
to get to uncertainty bands that
you're actually confident in.

00:48:32.400 --> 00:48:33.583
So we do that.

00:48:33.583 --> 00:48:35.250
We explore it from
the statistical side.

00:48:35.250 --> 00:48:37.083
But we also from the
business side just sort

00:48:37.083 --> 00:48:40.170
of take the
perspective of, here's

00:48:40.170 --> 00:48:41.520
what we've seen historically.

00:48:41.520 --> 00:48:43.780
You should expect some
uncertainty in the forecast.

00:48:43.780 --> 00:48:46.890
And then really digging in
when we do see forecast misses.

00:48:50.450 --> 00:48:52.300
Thank you.

00:48:52.300 --> 00:48:55.930
I saw on LinkedIn, I think it
was a post maybe from a year

00:48:55.930 --> 00:49:00.070
ago, where you're presenting on
managing a remote and globally

00:49:00.070 --> 00:49:03.040
distributed data science
team and was curious

00:49:03.040 --> 00:49:05.710
if you have any
tips that you'd like

00:49:05.710 --> 00:49:08.800
to share with us in terms
of managing a team that's

00:49:08.800 --> 00:49:10.340
all over.

00:49:10.340 --> 00:49:10.840
Yeah.

00:49:10.840 --> 00:49:12.215
And this was true
even pre-COVID.

00:49:12.215 --> 00:49:16.420
So I have folks in Dublin,
Ireland, in New York, DC--

00:49:16.420 --> 00:49:19.850
I'm in Minnesota--
folks in SF and Seattle.

00:49:19.850 --> 00:49:20.600
I think that's it.

00:49:20.600 --> 00:49:24.760
So we're very distributed,
have been for a while.

00:49:24.760 --> 00:49:27.070
I talked a bit about the
early stage technical feedback

00:49:27.070 --> 00:49:27.570
meetings.

00:49:27.570 --> 00:49:29.800
That has actually been a
surprisingly useful thing

00:49:29.800 --> 00:49:34.570
to do just for making sure that
we have that weekly touchpoint

00:49:34.570 --> 00:49:36.025
with the team.

00:49:36.025 --> 00:49:38.578
You can do Zoom happy hours
and those kinds of things.

00:49:38.578 --> 00:49:39.370
And those are nice.

00:49:39.370 --> 00:49:41.745
But it's nice to have a little
bit of structure and topic

00:49:41.745 --> 00:49:45.430
to what we're trying to do and
to be able to have somewhat

00:49:45.430 --> 00:49:49.780
of a technical conversation in
a very low-stakes environment.

00:49:49.780 --> 00:49:52.570
We don't expect you to have a
polished presentation, but much

00:49:52.570 --> 00:49:55.390
more of a rainstorm-type
atmosphere.

00:49:55.390 --> 00:49:59.488
So having that touchpoint
has been really nice.

00:49:59.488 --> 00:50:00.280
What else do we do?

00:50:00.280 --> 00:50:01.090
Snacks.

00:50:01.090 --> 00:50:02.110
People love snacks.

00:50:02.110 --> 00:50:05.770
So I will-- there are a number
of services that you can send

00:50:05.770 --> 00:50:07.720
snacks to people that just--

00:50:07.720 --> 00:50:11.050
I think there's a
certain nice feeling

00:50:11.050 --> 00:50:14.060
that people get by sharing
the same snacks on Zoom.

00:50:14.060 --> 00:50:14.770
It's a good one.

00:50:19.072 --> 00:50:22.820
I'm trying to remember what
are our other strategies.

00:50:22.820 --> 00:50:24.650
I think just-- oh,
focusing on onboarding,

00:50:24.650 --> 00:50:26.570
that's another big one.

00:50:26.570 --> 00:50:28.010
When folks start
remotely, that's

00:50:28.010 --> 00:50:30.020
a very different
experience than it

00:50:30.020 --> 00:50:33.260
used to be where they were
in the office among everybody

00:50:33.260 --> 00:50:35.880
in a starting class,
seeing everyone.

00:50:35.880 --> 00:50:38.660
So I focus really
closely on new folks.

00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:40.790
I meet with them every
day when they're starting.

00:50:40.790 --> 00:50:43.730
I set up meetings with
the relevant people

00:50:43.730 --> 00:50:44.690
that they need to meet.

00:50:44.690 --> 00:50:47.630
I make sure they have the
resources that they need.

00:50:47.630 --> 00:50:50.240
The first month or so, I
just focus really closely

00:50:50.240 --> 00:50:52.760
on new folks and making sure
they get integrated because it

00:50:52.760 --> 00:50:54.410
doesn't happen naturally.

00:50:54.410 --> 00:50:57.730
In a remote world, you
really have to work at that.

00:50:57.730 --> 00:51:00.490
Absolutely, I love the
point of even just sharing

00:51:00.490 --> 00:51:03.280
the same snacks, something
little like that.

00:51:03.280 --> 00:51:05.860
And Libby, I see you timed
your comment perfectly

00:51:05.860 --> 00:51:09.100
to when Dara was actually
talking about onboarding too.

00:51:09.100 --> 00:51:11.350
So just want to see if
you had any follow-up

00:51:11.350 --> 00:51:14.138
question on that, Libby?

00:51:14.138 --> 00:51:15.930
Yeah, that was really,
really well timed.

00:51:15.930 --> 00:51:18.880
I asked, and then
Dara answered it.

00:51:18.880 --> 00:51:19.380
Yeah.

00:51:19.380 --> 00:51:22.710
I think onboarding is
a really tough thing

00:51:22.710 --> 00:51:26.130
in data science in general,
and then extra tough

00:51:26.130 --> 00:51:27.720
when everybody's remote, right?

00:51:27.720 --> 00:51:32.340
And so I was kind of wondering
what emphasis you put on that

00:51:32.340 --> 00:51:34.590
and how it worked, and
if you buddied people up

00:51:34.590 --> 00:51:36.433
and things like that.

00:51:36.433 --> 00:51:38.100
But you really hit
the nail on the head.

00:51:38.100 --> 00:51:39.800
It has to be intentional.

00:51:39.800 --> 00:51:42.558
It's not going to happen
spontaneously, right?

00:51:42.558 --> 00:51:44.100
People aren't going
to just integrate

00:51:44.100 --> 00:51:47.068
into a group and seamlessly
work with everybody.

00:51:47.068 --> 00:51:48.610
There has to be some
intention there.

00:51:48.610 --> 00:51:50.190
So that was good to hear.

00:51:50.190 --> 00:51:53.200
If you have any expansion
on that, I'd love to hear.

00:51:53.200 --> 00:51:55.932
But if not, you did a
great job answering.

00:51:55.932 --> 00:51:57.390
Yeah, you hit
most of the points.

00:51:57.390 --> 00:51:58.870
We do have spin-up buddies.

00:51:58.870 --> 00:52:02.040
And so people get paired up
with an existing data scientist

00:52:02.040 --> 00:52:05.460
or data analyst that they
can meet with and ask

00:52:05.460 --> 00:52:06.960
any question to at any point.

00:52:06.960 --> 00:52:09.540
There's a Stripe-wide
onboarding curriculum

00:52:09.540 --> 00:52:11.700
that teaches people
about payments

00:52:11.700 --> 00:52:14.165
and the Stripe product.

00:52:14.165 --> 00:52:16.290
And then we have a data
science-specific curriculum

00:52:16.290 --> 00:52:18.540
as well, how to set
up your environment,

00:52:18.540 --> 00:52:20.730
how do we use Git at
Stripe, some of the more

00:52:20.730 --> 00:52:24.000
technical classes that we run.

00:52:24.000 --> 00:52:25.938
And those are both
pretty helpful.

00:52:25.938 --> 00:52:27.480
But yeah, from there,
it is very much

00:52:27.480 --> 00:52:29.750
just making sure to meet
with the right-- oh,

00:52:29.750 --> 00:52:31.000
and we have a spin-up project.

00:52:31.000 --> 00:52:33.333
So that's the other thing
that we do very intentionally.

00:52:33.333 --> 00:52:37.080
We plan a sort of
well-scoped project

00:52:37.080 --> 00:52:39.600
that we give to new folks
that should allow them

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:40.950
some room for exploration.

00:52:40.950 --> 00:52:42.575
It should be important
to the business,

00:52:42.575 --> 00:52:45.870
but not business critical,
need-it-next-week-type

00:52:45.870 --> 00:52:48.780
of project so that they can
ship something that is important

00:52:48.780 --> 00:52:51.540
but not feel that sort of
time pressure immediately.

00:52:51.540 --> 00:52:53.825
They need to have some time
to spin up on our systems

00:52:53.825 --> 00:52:54.450
and meet folks.

00:52:57.340 --> 00:52:58.000
That's great.

00:52:58.000 --> 00:53:00.400
Who comes up with that project?

00:53:00.400 --> 00:53:01.540
The manager does.

00:53:01.540 --> 00:53:04.180
OK.

00:53:04.180 --> 00:53:07.330
And do the newly
onboarded people

00:53:07.330 --> 00:53:10.058
work together on that and
share results with each other?

00:53:10.058 --> 00:53:12.100
[INAUDIBLE] side they
do because there's enough

00:53:12.100 --> 00:53:12.520
engineers.

00:53:12.520 --> 00:53:14.170
On the data science side,
it's typically just sort

00:53:14.170 --> 00:53:15.253
of one person per project.

00:53:18.430 --> 00:53:21.090
So I know we're getting
to the end of our time here,

00:53:21.090 --> 00:53:24.450
but one question I love to
ask people when we have time

00:53:24.450 --> 00:53:27.000
is, what is the
best career advice

00:53:27.000 --> 00:53:30.540
that you've ever received, or
maybe not best, but something

00:53:30.540 --> 00:53:32.573
that sticks in your mind?

00:53:32.573 --> 00:53:33.990
Something that
speaks to my mind

00:53:33.990 --> 00:53:37.650
is to think about choosing the
wild card when you're presented

00:53:37.650 --> 00:53:39.550
with multiple choices.

00:53:39.550 --> 00:53:43.090
So for me, when I was
choosing my PhD advisor,

00:53:43.090 --> 00:53:45.290
I had two rotations-- you
do three rotations, right?

00:53:45.290 --> 00:53:48.450
I had two rotations
that I had planned ahead

00:53:48.450 --> 00:53:51.240
of time that I knew I was very
interested in their research

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:53.880
that would go really
well, I thought.

00:53:53.880 --> 00:53:55.270
And they did both
go really well.

00:53:55.270 --> 00:53:56.520
And I really liked those labs.

00:53:56.520 --> 00:53:59.730
And then the third one was
this brand-new professor.

00:53:59.730 --> 00:54:01.320
I would be the
first grad student.

00:54:01.320 --> 00:54:03.320
It was a little bit outside
the kind of research

00:54:03.320 --> 00:54:04.420
that I had done before.

00:54:04.420 --> 00:54:08.470
So I thought, I'll just
do this wildcard rotation.

00:54:08.470 --> 00:54:11.700
And of course, that was the
lab that I ended up joining.

00:54:11.700 --> 00:54:13.770
I was the first grad
student and ended up

00:54:13.770 --> 00:54:17.640
having just this fantastic
experience with my advisor

00:54:17.640 --> 00:54:18.930
and with the research.

00:54:18.930 --> 00:54:22.080
And that also has held true
as I've made other career

00:54:22.080 --> 00:54:23.670
decisions along the way--

00:54:23.670 --> 00:54:26.220
choosing to go to Stripe
rather than stay in something

00:54:26.220 --> 00:54:28.020
in biology, choosing
to go to data science

00:54:28.020 --> 00:54:30.690
rather than do an
academic postdoc.

00:54:30.690 --> 00:54:34.350
Sometimes that
wildcard choice can

00:54:34.350 --> 00:54:36.810
feel a little scary, but
also kind of enticing.

00:54:36.810 --> 00:54:40.080
And for me, it's
just trying to not

00:54:40.080 --> 00:54:42.810
be afraid to choose that
option among choices

00:54:42.810 --> 00:54:46.010
has worked out well.

00:54:46.010 --> 00:54:46.650
Thank you.

00:54:46.650 --> 00:54:48.170
That's great advice.

00:54:48.170 --> 00:54:51.210
And Priyanka, I like
your comment in the chat,

00:54:51.210 --> 00:54:54.410
too, when I asked that
question to also frame it as,

00:54:54.410 --> 00:54:56.960
it could be advice
that you've been given

00:54:56.960 --> 00:54:59.540
or that you have given
somebody else too.

00:55:02.300 --> 00:55:04.610
I just wanted to
double check that I

00:55:04.610 --> 00:55:07.310
haven't missed anybody's
questions in the chat

00:55:07.310 --> 00:55:08.390
or on Slido.

00:55:08.390 --> 00:55:12.065
So feel free to raise your hand
here or just unmute as well.

00:55:15.640 --> 00:55:20.110
I'll give people a
few seconds here.

00:55:20.110 --> 00:55:24.260
But, Dara, when you were talking
about some of the conversations

00:55:24.260 --> 00:55:27.320
you have with your direct
reports on their career growth,

00:55:27.320 --> 00:55:30.140
you mentioned some recommended
reading or resources

00:55:30.140 --> 00:55:31.010
that you give them.

00:55:31.010 --> 00:55:32.595
And I was curious
if that's something

00:55:32.595 --> 00:55:34.220
you could share with
us, or if you have

00:55:34.220 --> 00:55:36.540
any books that you'd recommend?

00:55:36.540 --> 00:55:37.040
Sure.

00:55:40.580 --> 00:55:43.580
At Stripe, there's often
manager workshops or trainings.

00:55:43.580 --> 00:55:46.430
And so I'm constantly
in a state of learning

00:55:46.430 --> 00:55:49.185
as well about things.

00:55:49.185 --> 00:55:51.560
So I'll just share a couple
that are sort of top of mind.

00:55:51.560 --> 00:55:57.620
There's lots of-- for books on
management and actually just

00:55:57.620 --> 00:55:59.896
general career books,
one of my favorites is--

00:55:59.896 --> 00:56:03.410
what's it called-- The Manager's
Path by Camille Fournier.

00:56:03.410 --> 00:56:07.170
And she was the CTO
at Rent the Runway.

00:56:07.170 --> 00:56:10.190
And so she takes you through how
you can think about your career

00:56:10.190 --> 00:56:13.040
from as a manager from
managing an intern,

00:56:13.040 --> 00:56:15.650
to managing a few
folks, managing a larger

00:56:15.650 --> 00:56:18.680
team, all the way up, up,
up to being an executive.

00:56:18.680 --> 00:56:21.380
I think that's very useful
both for seasoned managers

00:56:21.380 --> 00:56:26.740
to understand how to think about
your career at various levels.

00:56:26.740 --> 00:56:29.920
It's certainly one that I
revisit from time to time

00:56:29.920 --> 00:56:32.410
as I've moved through my career.

00:56:32.410 --> 00:56:38.200
So I find that one
to be super useful.

00:56:38.200 --> 00:56:43.280
The High Output Management book
by Andy Grove is a classic.

00:56:43.280 --> 00:56:46.350
It's one that we reference
a lot at Stripe as well.

00:56:46.350 --> 00:56:48.100
These, again, are more
management focused,

00:56:48.100 --> 00:56:51.950
but I think good resources for
thinking about your career.

00:56:51.950 --> 00:56:54.767
One this framework
that I have been

00:56:54.767 --> 00:56:56.350
using in career
conversations recently

00:56:56.350 --> 00:56:57.308
is kind of interesting.

00:56:57.308 --> 00:57:01.810
It's called the SCARF framework
it's a neurobiology thing.

00:57:01.810 --> 00:57:05.930
And it's about how you respond
to stressful situations.

00:57:05.930 --> 00:57:08.312
So it's like--

00:57:08.312 --> 00:57:10.520
I'm not sure if I can remember
what they all are now.

00:57:10.520 --> 00:57:12.560
But it's things
like, connectedness,

00:57:12.560 --> 00:57:16.130
autonomy, relationships,
and thinking about what's

00:57:16.130 --> 00:57:18.210
important to you in your work.

00:57:18.210 --> 00:57:20.660
And so I use that and
ask people to talk

00:57:20.660 --> 00:57:23.930
about what resonates with
them in the SCARF framework.

00:57:23.930 --> 00:57:25.880
And it's funny how
different people really

00:57:25.880 --> 00:57:28.252
value different things
when it comes to their work

00:57:28.252 --> 00:57:29.460
and what's important to them.

00:57:29.460 --> 00:57:31.850
And so I found that a
very helpful framework

00:57:31.850 --> 00:57:34.910
for learning about folks
and for folks to self

00:57:34.910 --> 00:57:37.920
identify what's
important to them.

00:57:37.920 --> 00:57:38.420
Thank you.

00:57:38.420 --> 00:57:39.695
I'm trying to find the link.

00:57:39.695 --> 00:57:40.550
So it's Scarf--

00:57:40.550 --> 00:57:41.756
S-C-A-R-F?

00:57:41.756 --> 00:57:43.040
Uh-huh.

00:57:43.040 --> 00:57:44.960
OK.

00:57:44.960 --> 00:57:47.660
I like to share some
of the links as we go.

00:57:47.660 --> 00:57:53.210
And the book that you
said by Andy Grove, what

00:57:53.210 --> 00:57:54.398
was the name of that one?

00:57:54.398 --> 00:57:55.440
High Output Management.

00:57:55.440 --> 00:57:56.630
High Output Management.

00:57:56.630 --> 00:57:57.230
OK.

00:57:57.230 --> 00:57:59.240
Awesome.

